title: "Python SDK"
description: "Use pyqql for native QQL parsing, policy injection, planning, and Qdrant execution."
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

{% terminal title="Install" %}
pip install pyqql
{% /terminal %}

{% qqlExample title="Input used below" %}
QUERY TEXT 'cardiology' FROM medical_records
USING dense
LIMIT 5;
{% /qqlExample %}

```python
import pyqql

client = pyqql.Client("http://localhost:6333")
report = client.execute(query)
print(report.ok, report.succeeded, report.failed)
```

## Parse and isolate

```python
stmt = pyqql.parse(query)[0]
pyqql.inject_filter(stmt, "tenant_id", "=", "hospital-7")
stmt.shard_key = "hospital-7"
report = client.execute(stmt)
```

The native binding returns statement handles. Free-standing and client
execution support strings, statement objects, and host arrays. Async variants
are available for event-loop applications.

{% aside type="note" title="Edge package" %}
Install `pyqql-edge` only when you need the in-process edge backend and bundled
embedding support. The regular package has the smaller remote-client surface.
{% /aside %}
